::Q1:: Why is PySpark particularly useful when processing very large datasets? {
=It can distribute data and computation across multiple machines in a cluster
~It converts all large datasets into Pandas DataFrames before processing
~It stores the entire dataset permanently in the SparkSession
~It eliminates the need for any data partitioning during processing
}

::Q2:: What is one of the main benefits of Spark's in-memory processing capability? {
=It can reduce disk I/O by keeping frequently used data in memory
~It eliminates the need to distribute data across worker nodes
~It automatically converts all Spark DataFrames into Pandas DataFrames
~It allows Spark to process data without using any computing resources
}

::Q3:: Which statement best explains the relationship between Apache Spark and PySpark? {
=Apache Spark provides the distributed processing engine, while PySpark provides a Python interface for using Spark
~PySpark is a separate distributed processing engine that replaces Apache Spark
~Apache Spark is a Python library built on top of PySpark
~PySpark is only used to convert Spark applications written in Scala into Python
}